[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read what the reports
show and it holds two problems. The queued parser fix is aimed at one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

**`swap`: day and month come out swapped.** Booked 2 March, invited for 3 February. Every
tenant on file writes the day first, and the date is read month first. The kb's own logic
(`causes.ts`) splits these reports into `parser` and `hint` by tenant and channel, but every one
of them shows the same thing, and the reporters cannot tell which way in they used. One problem,
one ticket: whoever fixes it checks both the shared parser and the booking-v3 form's
`MM/DD/YYYY` hint.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day.** Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; a client in
Lisbon gets Tuesday and a client in Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting; an invite arrives
before the booking. Nothing is swapped. The desk noted these reports by hand
(`desk-notes.json`) and still counted them under `parser` by tenant. They point at time zones,
not at date order, and the parser fix will not close them.

## How they were counted

- One report, one id: the ticket export repeats some reports across pages, so duplicates are
  dropped before counting.
- By what the report shows, not by intake's tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser and
  is wrong often enough that the tag cannot be trusted as a count.
- Not by subject: the same subject line covers both problems
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone; that is how
  both ended up in one ticket).
- Every number on this page is computed from the export when the page renders, so it follows
  the data as the desk keeps working.

